# Region allocation with `stdlib.arena`

An arena groups short-lived allocations into blocks. Values are addressed by
small integer IDs, so callers can keep those IDs while the arena grows. Reset
the arena once the whole batch is finished instead of releasing each value
individually.

## Constructing an arena

`arena.new(capacity)` starts with one empty block. `arena.default()` uses a
capacity of 1024. The capacity is the number of values per block, not a byte
size.

```ltl
import stdlib.arena

let a = arena.new(3)
let defaults = arena.default()
```

## Allocating and rolling over blocks

Allocation returns `[updated_arena, slot_id]`. Keep the updated arena from the
first item in the pair. The ID is `block_index * capacity + slot_index`.

```ltl
let result = arena.alloc(a, "first")
let a = result[0]
let first_id = result[1]

let batch = arena.alloc_many(a, ["second", "third", "fourth"])
let a = batch[0]
let ids = batch[1]
```

With capacity three, the fourth value starts block two. `total(a)` reports four,
`block_count(a)` reports two, and `remaining(a)` reports two because the new
block contains one value.

## Reading and updating values

Use `get` and `set` with the returned IDs. `get` returns `nil` for an ID that is
past the live values or negative; it never turns an invalid read into an
accidental last-element lookup.

```ltl
let value = arena.get(a, first_id)
let a = arena.set(a, first_id, "updated")
assert(arena.get(a, first_id) == "updated")
assert(arena.get(a, 999) == nil)
assert(arena.get(a, -1) == nil)
```

## Pinned test vector

The complete executable vector is
`examples/arena_pinned_vector.ltl`. It pins construction, single and batch
allocation, rollover, IDs, reads, writes, totals, block counts, remaining
capacity, defaults, and both forms of out-of-range read. Run it from the
repository root:

```text
lateralus run examples/arena_pinned_vector.ltl
```

It ends with `pinned vector: PASS` after all assertions succeed.

## Resetting a region

When a batch is complete, `arena.reset(a)` returns the arena to one empty block
and zero total allocations. This makes the lifetime boundary explicit and
keeps cleanup proportional to the number of blocks, rather than the number of
individual values.

